    "summary": "Create a visual map of the institutions, roles, places, relationships, systems, and social conditions that shape how you encounter society. Explain two routes through the map and how your place in society affects what you notice or may overlook.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>SOC122, Fall 2026. Weight: 20 percent.</strong></p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Create a visual map of the institutions, roles, places, relationships, systems, and social conditions that shape how you encounter society. Explain two routes through the map and how your place in society affects what you notice or may overlook.</p><h3>What to submit</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One readable visual map.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A 500 to 700 word explanation.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One source of access or advantage and one barrier or limit.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One course concept and one question your position makes easier or harder to see.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A short privacy note naming what you chose not to include.</li></ul><h3>Boundary</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You control the level of disclosure. Identity proof, trauma, private family history, and legal or financial details are not required.</p><h3>How your work will be graded</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Strong means 85 to 100 percent of a criterion, Clear means 70 to 84 percent, Developing means 50 to 69 percent, and Limited means 0 to 49 percent.</p><h3>Map evidence: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> Institutions, roles, places, relationships, and forces form a readable system.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The main map elements and pathways are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> The map is partial or weakly connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The map cannot be used to understand your position.</li></ul><h3>Structural analysis: 6 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> Access, advantage, constraint, and visibility are analysed without reducing them to individual choice.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The main structural effects are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Analysis is mostly descriptive or individualised.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> Structural analysis is absent or unsupported.</li></ul><h3>Course concept and question: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> A concept sharpens a thoughtful question about what the position reveals or hides.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The concept and question are relevant and clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> The connection is thin or generic.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The concept or question is absent or substantially inaccurate.</li></ul><h3>Communication and privacy: 4 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The visual and explanation are coherent, accessible, and use intentional privacy boundaries.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The work is clear and respects privacy.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Clarity or privacy choices are uneven.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The work is difficult to follow or requires inappropriate disclosure.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your grade is based on the evidence in your work. Personal disclosure, polished production, and technical effects do not replace course learning.</p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select <strong>Start attempt</strong>. Write or paste your written work into the <strong>Submission</strong> editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Insert your required visual into the editor with a text description, or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select <strong>Submit</strong>, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Released:</strong> September 18, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due:</strong> October 2, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
