    "html": "<p>Worth 10% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 10 points.</p><h3>Purpose</h3><p>Complete two dated entries. Begin with a specific item from class, public issue, or low-risk observation. Explain what one social science approach reveals. Then use a specific Indigenous or Two-Eyed Seeing course source to show what changes, expands, or remains outside that account.</p><h3>Each entry must include</h3><ul><li>A date and specific observation.</li><li>The social science discipline or method used first.</li><li>A named Indigenous or Two-Eyed Seeing course source.</li><li>What becomes visible, changes, or remains unresolved when the sources are held together.</li><li>One limit, question, or next step.</li></ul><h3>Boundary</h3><p>Do not invent an Indigenous perspective, treat different Indigenous Nations as if they have one shared view, or speak for a community. Use the assigned author or organization accurately.</p><h3>How Set 1 is graded</h3><ul><li>Dated observation evidence: 2.5 points.</li><li>source accuracy and respect: 3 points.</li><li>analysis using both sources of knowledge: 3 points.</li><li>communication and limits: 1.5 points.</li></ul><h3>How to post your journal entries</h3><ul><li>Open this journal in Blackboard and find <strong>Entries</strong>. Start a separate entry for each required date or example.</li><li>Write directly in the journal editor. Put the entry number and date at the top. You do not need to upload a Word document.</li><li>If you choose an audio entry, attach the recording and a transcript, or add a Seneca-accessible recording link.</li><li>Select <strong>Post</strong>, then reopen the entry to confirm it is there. You may edit your entries before the due date. Only you and the teaching team can read this journal.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p>Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Privacy and accessibility</h3><p>You control what personal information you share. Remove identifying details about other people. Use readable headings and descriptive links. Add a text description for meaningful visuals, and captions or a transcript for audio or video. Your grade reflects your learning and reasoning; private disclosure and production effects do not earn extra marks.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p><strong>Released:</strong> September 18, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p><strong>Due:</strong> October 23, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
