    "title": "3. Research Choices Lab (20%)",
    "name": "Research Choices Lab",
    "summary": "Explore how research choices affect what we can learn and whose experiences are included. Complete three fictional scenarios, explain your reasoning, and propose a small ethical study. You are designing a study only: do not recruit people, collect private information, or carry out the study.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Explore how research choices affect what we can learn and whose experiences are included. Complete three fictional scenarios, explain your reasoning, and propose a small ethical study. You are designing a study only: do not recruit people, collect private information, or carry out the study.</p><h3>Part A: three research choices</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Read each scenario below and select one option. There is no answer key and these selections are worth zero points. Several choices can be justified. Your written explanation is graded on how well you explain your decision, its benefits, its risks, and its limits.</p><h3>Explain each choice</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">For each scenario, write 3 to 5 focused sentences. Identify your chosen option, explain why you would start there, and name one benefit and one limitation or risk. Use a course idea about evidence, research methods, consent, or interpretation where it helps you explain your reasoning.</p><h3>Part B: propose a study</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Choose a public or course-related issue. Write one question that a small study could address.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Name a method, such as a survey, interviews, observation of a public setting, or analysis of public documents. Explain why it fits your question and who or what you would study.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Explain what participation would involve, how consent and privacy would be protected, who might benefit, and who should help shape or review the work.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If your question concerns a specific Indigenous Nation or community, use assigned sources to identify why the relevant community’s authority and permission matter. Do not invent permission or speak for that community.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Name two limits. Explain what your proposed evidence could support and what it could not establish.</li></ul><h3>How your work is graded: 20 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Three scenario explanations: 6 points total, 2 points each.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Research question, method, and study plan: 6 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Consent, privacy, benefits, and accountability: 5 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limits and clear communication: 3 points.</li></ul><h3>How to complete and submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open the assessment and select <strong>Start attempt</strong>. For each scenario, use the numbered selection control to choose one option. You can change your selection while the attempt is open.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Write your answers in the response editors in this assessment. You do not need to upload a document.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Check each selection and written response before selecting <strong>Submit</strong>. Wait for the confirmation and keep your submission receipt.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Privacy and accessibility</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You control what personal information you share. Remove identifying details about other people. Use readable headings and descriptive links. Add a text description for meaningful visuals, and captions or a transcript for audio or video. Your grade reflects your learning and reasoning; private disclosure and production effects do not earn extra marks.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Released:</strong> October 16, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due:</strong> November 6, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
