    "name": "Reconciliation in Practice: A Community Question",
    "summary": "Choose one real public or local issue connected to reconciliation. Create a respectful community question and a practical next step. Support both with evidence and identify who has the authority to act.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 15% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 15 points.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>SOC122, Fall 2026. Weight: 15 percent.</strong></p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose one real public or local issue connected to reconciliation. Create a respectful community question and a practical next step. Support both with evidence and identify who has the authority to act.</p><h3>What to submit</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A specific and manageable public or local issue.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One researchable community question.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One Indigenous-led or official community source and one relevant social science source.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">The person, organization, or institution able to act.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A responsible next step, one limit, and one step that requires a relationship with, or permission from, the relevant community.</li></ul><h3>Format</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose 800 to 1,000 words or a 5 to 6 minute narrated brief with a one-page source sheet. Do not speak for Indigenous peoples or communities.</p><h3>How your work will be graded</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Strong means 85 to 100 percent of a criterion, Clear means 70 to 84 percent, Developing means 50 to 69 percent, and Limited means 0 to 49 percent.</p><h3>Question and scope: 3.75 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The issue and question are specific, researchable, and manageable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The issue and question are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Scope is broad or the question is only partly researchable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The question is unclear or inappropriate.</li></ul><h3>Source authority: 4.5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> Indigenous-led or official community evidence and social science evidence are accurate and well integrated.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> Required source types support the brief.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Source use is thin or uneven.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> Key claims lack appropriate authority.</li></ul><h3>Responsible action: 4.5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The acting audience, next step, authority, permission, and limits form a credible path.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The next step and responsible actor are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> The action is generic or weakly connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The proposal speaks for others or lacks a responsible path.</li></ul><h3>Communication: 2.25 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The brief is clear, respectful, and supported by sources you identify.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> Communication is clear overall.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Communication is uneven.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The work is difficult to follow.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your grade is based on the evidence in your work. Personal disclosure, polished production, and technical effects do not replace course learning.</p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select <strong>Start attempt</strong>. Write or paste your written work into the <strong>Submission</strong> editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Insert your required visual into the editor with a text description, or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select <strong>Submit</strong>, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Released:</strong> November 6, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due:</strong> December 4, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
