    "id": "personal-cartography",
    "title": "6. Personal Cartography: Society and Me (15%)",
    "name": "Personal Cartography: Society and Me",
    "summary": "Create a five-point route showing how your social analysis changed during the course and what responsible participation in society now requires from you.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 15% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 15 points.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>SOC122, Fall 2026. Weight: 15 percent.</strong></p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Create a five-point route showing how your social analysis changed during the course and what responsible participation in society now requires from you.</p><h3>Required route points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">An early assumption or question.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Evidence that complicated your view.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">How two social science disciplines changed the question.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">What Two-Eyed Seeing required you to reconsider.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One realistic action you can take as an informed and responsible member of society.</li></ul><h3>Format</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose a 5 to 7 minute narrated map or a 900 to 1,200 word visual essay. Use at least four dated items from your course work. The personal element is your documented learning, not private disclosure.</p><h3>How your work will be graded</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Strong means 85 to 100 percent of a criterion, Clear means 70 to 84 percent, Developing means 50 to 69 percent, and Limited means 0 to 49 percent.</p><h3>Course-long evidence: 4.5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> Four or more dated course items create a convincing route through the course.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> Required course items are present and connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Evidence is thin, clustered, or weakly connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The map lacks required course evidence.</li></ul><h3>Change in social analysis: 3.75 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The map shows specific changes in questions, disciplines, and interpretation.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> A clear change in analysis is shown.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> Change is claimed but weakly demonstrated.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> No meaningful development is shown.</li></ul><h3>Two-Eyed Seeing and limits: 3.75 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> You accurately explain what Two-Eyed Seeing changed and what remains outside your authority.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The connection and limits are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> The connection is general or incomplete.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The framework is misrepresented or limits are ignored.</li></ul><h3>Communication and citizenship: 3 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Strong:</strong> The route is coherent, accessible, and ends with a realistic act of informed citizenship.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Clear:</strong> The route and future action are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Developing:</strong> The route or action is vague.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Limited:</strong> The work is difficult to follow or lacks a future action.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your grade is based on the evidence in your work. Personal disclosure, polished production, and technical effects do not replace course learning.</p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select <strong>Start attempt</strong>. Write or paste your written work into the <strong>Submission</strong> editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Insert your required visual into the editor with a text description, or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select <strong>Submit</strong>, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Released:</strong> December 4, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due:</strong> December 11, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
