    "id": "final-reflection",
    "title": "7. Final Learning Reflection (10%)",
    "name": "Final Learning Reflection",
    "summary": "Look back at how your understanding of society developed. Use two pieces of your own course work to show what you now notice, question, or explain differently. This is a short comparison of your learning, not a second Personal Cartography project.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 10% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 10 points.</p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Look back at how your understanding of society developed. Use two pieces of your own course work to show what you now notice, question, or explain differently. This is a short comparison of your learning, not a second Personal Cartography project.</p><h3>Choose your evidence</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Choose one dated piece of your own work from Weeks 1 to 7 and one from Weeks 8 to 13. You can use an assignment, journal entry, or dated course notes.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">For each piece, give its title or description, date, and the exact section or example you are discussing. Quote one short phrase or describe a specific detail.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Use work you already have. You do not need new research, new interviews, another map, or feedback on your final assignments.</li></ul><h3>1. What did you think or do earlier?</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use the earlier piece to explain how you first understood a social issue, relationship, or everyday situation. What did you notice, assume, or leave out?</p><h3>2. What do you understand differently now?</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Compare the earlier piece with your later work. Explain a specific change or a question that became more complicated. Use two relevant concepts from this course to explain the comparison. Explain the concepts in your own words and identify the assigned source or week. If you discuss an Indigenous teaching or perspective, identify its source and do not speak for a Nation or community.</p><h3>3. What will you carry forward?</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Name one question or realistic action you will carry into your studies, work, or community life. Explain how your course evidence supports that next step and what you still need to understand.</p><h3>Length and format</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Aim for 400 to 600 words across the three responses, or a 3 to 5 minute audio response with a transcript. Include a short evidence list identifying the two pieces of your work and the course sources or weeks you used. The evidence list is not part of the suggested word count.</p><h3>How your reflection is graded: 10 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Specific evidence from your own course work: 3 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Accurate use of course concepts: 3 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Change in understanding and a next step: 3 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear communication and privacy: 1 point.</li></ul><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select Start attempt. Write or paste your three responses into the Submission editor, using the three numbered headings above. No Word upload is required.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If you choose audio, attach the recording and transcript in this assignment, or add a working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link that I can open.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Check that all three responses and your evidence list are included. You do not need to re-upload your earlier assignments.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select Submit, wait for the confirmation, and keep the receipt. Saving a draft does not submit your work.</li></ul><h3>Privacy and accessibility</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You do not need to disclose trauma, health information, family circumstances, or another person's private information. You may discuss a public, course-based, or fictional example. Your grade is based on evidence and reasoning, not personal disclosure or a claim that you improved.</p><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own course work and explanations. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite this reflection, invent a learning experience, or create narration in your place. Spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and other accessibility tools are welcome when they do not generate your course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Release and due dates</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Released: November 30, 2026 at 12:00 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Due: December 13, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</p>",
